## Engineering Donor-acceptor Heterostructure Crystals for Photonic Logic Computation
Dedicated to the 100th anniversary of Nankai University
## Introduction
Photonic materials are emerging as an active research field thanks to their broad utility in functional devices including sensing, anti-counterfeiting, to three-dimensional (3D) data storage, photon upconversion, lasing and nonlinear optics.[1-7] In recent years, manipulating photons in complex heterostructures with segregated domains has become essential due to their potential applications for the simple integration of photonic devices with novel functionalities.[8-11] The distinct intrinsic character of the domains with different components allows for highly tunable energy/charge transfer and enhanced charge carrier mobility, which results in the interesting photonic properties of heterostructures.[12-14] On the other hand, the morphology of these heterostructures can greatly influence the confinement propagation and optical behaviors by reflecting, refracting, etc.[13] Therefore, the rational design and modulation of heterostructures will provide a powerful tool for realizing more complicated integrated photonic functionalities.
## RESEARCH ARTICLE
Since the structure and composition of heterostructures play critical roles in optimizing optical properties, crystalline materials are an ideal platform for study thanks to their highly crystalline nature and well-defined structures, which ensures straightforward structure and component tuning.[15-17] Furthermore, crystallinity makes it feasible to determine the relationship between structure and optical properties. Extensive efforts have been devoted to the rational design and modulation of crystalline heterostructures in order to improve their optical properties.[18-20] Especially, the study organic donor-acceptor (D-A) systems with charge-transfer (CT) interactions has emerged as an important and rapidly growing area of research.[21-23] Since electrons are mostly delocalized from the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) of donors to the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) of acceptors in the CT process, the tuning of the CT-based emission of the organic D-A system can be achieved through bandgap engineering by rational choice of D and A species and modulation of their packing modes.[24-26] However, considering the complicated factors that may affect crystallization processes, the modulation of the structure and composition of organic crystals at the molecular level remains a great challenge.[27] Also, the morphology of these crystalline heterostructures is mainly controlled by relatively weak intermolecular interactions during crystal growth, which can be readily affected by external factors, such as the solvent, concentration gradients, temperature and humidity.[27] Therefore, controlling morphology may lead to an unintended result due to the unpredictability of these external factors.Crystalline organic/inorganic hybrid materials prepared by coordination-directed assembly, such as metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) or coordination polymers, have become one of the most important classes of advanced materials thanks to their crystallinity, tunable struc