Crystalline organic/inorganic hybrid materials prepared by coordination-directed  assembly,  such  as  metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) or coordination polymers, have become one of the most important classes of advanced materials thanks to their crystallinity, tunable structures and variable compositions.[28- 32] On the basis of these advantages, we have developed a new strategy for the rational construction of crystalline D-A materials utilizing a host-guest MOF as a platform.[33] Herein, we further develop  the  strategy  by  aiming  at  the  preparation  and characterisation of a rich library of donor-acceptor heterostructure crystals. Specifically, the D or A moieties can be first introduced into the host framework as the ligand and the A or D guests can be incorporated into the host matrix during or after crystallisation. D-A crystals can be directly assembled based on the synergy of coordination and D-A interactions. Macroscopic heterostructures are then accessed by using these D-A crystals as seeds and heteroepitaxial growth. Since this strategy allows for relatively independent modulation of the host and guest components with various D and A moieties, the engineering of the heterostructures with this strategy can be quite flexible. In our work, NKU-111, which is constructed with the electron-deficient 2,4,6-tri(pyridin-4- yl)-1,6,5-thiazine (py) ligand do the A component, was selected as the MOF host based on our previous research of crystalline D-A materials.[33] Then, a series of hierarchical crystals were obtained through epitaxial growth and stepwise introduction of different electron-rich polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) guests as D components. These hierarchical crystals exhibit anisotropic growth due to the anisotropic structure of the NKU-111 host. Therefore, the morphology of the hierarchical crystals can be rationally modulated by controlling the reaction conditions. Domain dependent emission tuning is readily achieved within these hierarchical crystals based on spatially segregated guest
dependent D-A systems. In addition, spatially mono-directional energy transfer between domains can be achieved through the HOMO-LUMO engineering of different D-A systems and the wellmatched heterointerface of the hierarchical crystals. Based on these characteristics, the resulting hierarchical crystals can be used as an optical logic gate, demonstrating their potential in integrated photonic logic circuitry.
## Results and Discussion
Design and construction. For the construction of D-A heterostructures using MOFs featuring host-guest capabilities, the two main essential factors are confined space within the host matrix and strong D-A interactions. This will greatly influence the affinity of the guests and the stability of the resulting D-A systems. In the NKU-111 host matrix, the cage-based porous network and electron-deficient tpt ligands (Figure 1a) allow electron-rich guest molecules (Figure 1b) to be encapsulated into separated cages in the host MOF framework. These guest molecules are arranged in a face to face π-π stacking mode with tpt ligands, resulting in strong D-A interactions (Figure 1c).[33] In addition, guest-dependent NKU-111 exhibits enhanced stability and the guest molecules can be trapped in the lattice, allowing for the possibility of using a stepwise assembly method to synthesize spatially segregated heterostructures. The obtained crystals are referred to as PAH@NKU-111 (PAH = anthracene for 1, phenanthrene for 2, pyrene for 3, triphenylene for 4, perylene for 5 and coronene for 6, respectively, as shown in Figure 1b.) The BFDH crystal morphology calculation based on their crystal structure shows a hexagonal morphology with dominant growth direction along the [001] direction (Figure 1d), which correlates well with that of the sample obtained (Figure 1e). The host framework shows a typical anisotropic crystalline feature. The daylight and fluorescence images of these PAH@NKU-111 crystals are displayed in Figure S1. These crystals show obvious eye-detectable colors and
adsorption bands in the visible region (Figure S2). The wide emission ranging from blue-green to red fluorescence reveals a guest-dependent emission behavior resulting from the effective charge-transfer between PAHs and tpt (Figure S2).
The confined space and strong D-A stacking in PAH@NKU-111 motivated us to synthesize a family of heterostructure crystals. These hierarchical crystals can be generated by an epitaxial growing process starting from the PAH@NKU-111. Extended domains were propagated on the pristine domain, resulting in b@a@NKU-111 with three domains, where a is the guest in the pristine part and b is the guest in the extended part (Figure 2a-f and Figure S3). Based on such a simple and effective method, heterostructure crystals containing three-component guests and five domains can also be engineered by otherwise epitaxial growing process, which can be referred to as c@b@a@NKU-111 (Figure 2g-j). We note that the pristine encapsulated guest molecules did not leak during the epitaxial growth process, which we attribute to the strong D-A interactions. The isotropic growth of the extended domain on the pristine domain is foreseeable due to their well-matched structures.[33] Indeed, these heterostructure crystals display exactly the same unit cell parameters and PXRD patterns as the initial seeds, which further confirm their sequentially periodic organization and high phase purity (Table S1 and Figure S4). The images of these heterostructure crystals
reveal the formation of long hexagonal prisms as a result of the isotropic growth of the extended layer on the pristine layer. It is interesting to note that the striped-like model is commonly observed from the epitaxial growth in this system. Accordingly, it can be concluded that the growth rate along the [001] direction might surpass the hexagonal facet perpendicular to the [001] direction, which favors the anisotropic epitaxial growth of the striped-like predominant hierarchical crystals.
Domain emission tuning. Using the aforementioned anisotropic epitaxial growth process, a series of heterostructure crystals were obtained and these crystals showed CT-based emission and guest-dependent domain emissive properties. For all hierarchical crystals, the segregated domains could be distinguished readily on the basis of their distinctive colors. The hierarchical crystals showed more clearly segregated multicolor luminescence under a fluorescence microscope (Figure 2 and S3). It is worth noting that 1@NKU-111, 3@NKU-111, 5@NKU-111 and 6@NKU-111 have broad absorption bands from 350 to \(550\;\mathsf{nm}\), while 2@NKU-111 and 4@NKU-111 only show absorption bands in the range from 350 to \(450\;\mathsf{nm}\) (Figure S2). This significant difference indicates that the combination of 1@NKU-111, 3@NKU-111, 5@NKU-111, 6@NKU-111 with 2@NKU-111, 4@NKU-111 will result in excitation and domain dependent optical response. To verify our supposition, a series of experiments were conducted.
