Taking 4@5@NKU-111 and 5@4@NKU-111 as examples, when excited by green light (515-560 nm), intense red emission was found only in the pristine domain of 4@5@NKU-111 and in the extended domains of 5@4@NKU-111. Instead, when excited with UV light (340-380 nm), strong blue-green fluorescence and relatively weak red emission were simultaneously detected (Figure 2a, b). Therefore, it was possible to selectively and independently switch the domain emission behavior by a careful choice of the excitation wavelength. In addition, the domain emission properties can also be tuned by changing the guest molecules resulting from the guest-dependent emission behavior
(Figure 2 and S3). This intriguing behavior establishes that we can fabricate multi-luminescent heterostructure crystals via heteroepitaxial growth based on CT interactions between donors and the acceptor. It is noteworthy that, upon green light excitation, the fluorescence microscopy images for the striped-like 4@5@NKU-111 and 2@5@NKU-111 crystals showed brighter red emission at both tips of each microrod (Figure 2a, c), which is a typical active optical waveguide feature. This phenomenon suggests that photons could be propagated along the crystal and lead to exciting photonic behaviors, which will be discussed in detail in the following parts.
Morphology modulation. Crystal features such as size and morphology can be feasibly tuned through the anisotropic coordination mode of crystalline hybrid materials and controlling the coordination interactions between metal ions and organic linkers.[34,35] In particular, the preparation of multifunctional crystals with controlled morphology has become an emerging topic to meet the specific requirements for photonic, electronic, and optoelectronic applications. In this case, the framework of the PAH@NKU-111 was connected by 1, 4-benzenedicarboxylate (BDC²) ligands along the [001] direction and extended by tpt ligands along the hexagonal facets perpendicular to the [001] direction (Figure 1c). It is possible to add blocking agents with the same chemical functionality as the ligands, which may generate a competitive situation to regulate the growth rate of certain
special facet and framework. Accordingly, the crystal morphology can be controlled by this method. As for the heterostructure crystals, this may also be achieved under the premise of successfully tuning the morphology of seed crystals.
For proof of concept, systematic reactions using benzoic acid as a competitive agent with BDC² ligands were used to modulate the morphology of crystals based on 4@NKU-111 and 5@NKU-111. Hexagonal rod, hexagonal lump and hexagonal sheet seed crystals were obtained, respectively, with the increasing amount of benzoic acid added into the reaction system (Figure S5). As the BFDH morphology simulation reveals, the dimension of these crystals along the [001] direction is dominated by the BDC²-Cd²+ coordination assembly (Figure 1c, d). The benzoic acid dependent morphologies of the crystals confirm our supposition
