that the relative growth rate along the [001] direction becomes slower with the increasing amount of benzoic acid, offering effective morphology modulation. As a result, striped-like, core-shell and sheet-like 4@5@NKU-111 heterocrystals were then generated, respectively, by using 5@NKU-111 crystals with different morphologies as seeds and different amounts of benzoic acid (Figure 3). These results suggest that the morphology of the heterocrystals could also be readily modulated through this method.
Energy transfer between spatially segregated domains. Energy transfer between adjacent domains is an effective way for optical signal manipulation in D-A heterostructure crystals, which can take place between adjacent domains with appropriate emission-induced energy gradient.[11] In our systems, because of the confined space and the face-to-face π-π stacking mode of PAH donors and tpt acceptors, the emission-induced energy of specific PAH-tpt D-A system has a positive correlation with the HOMO orbital level of PAH molecules. In addition, the nature of planar molecular structures of donors will not allow for configurational changes when encapsulated into the host framework, which ensures that the HOMO orbital level of the guest PAH in host matrix is nearly the same as that in free state. Accordingly, the HOMO-LUMO gap of the D-A system in each domain could be rationally regulated based on the ionization potentials (IPs) of PAHs.[36] Subsequently, the energy gap gradient between adjacent domains could be engineered to facilitate the spatial energy transfer between them.
According to our earlier studies,[33,36] triphenylene (4) and perylene (5) have distinctive IPs, so spatial energy transfer may
occur effectively between the domains in 4@5@NKU-111. A series of experiments and theoretical calculations were performed to determine the spatial energy transfer feasibility. First, the UV absorption and fluorescence emission spectra of 4@NKU-111 and 5@NKU-111 were recorded (Figure 4a). The emission spectrum of 4@NKU-111 totally overlaps with the absorption spectrum of 5@NKU-111 (represented by the solid green line and the dotted red line, respectively), which indicates the potential of efficient energy transfer from 4@NKU-111 domain to 5@NKU-111 domain. Theoretical calculations were then performed to gain further support of the potential for efficient energy transfer processes (Figure 4b). The energy gap of the CT complex mainly results from the HOMO of the PAH molecules and the LUMO of the tpt ligand in PAH@NKU-111, which was calculated to be 3.2 eV for 4@NKU-111 and 2.3 eV for 5@NKU-111, respectively. These results agree well with the experimental absorption spectra. Overall, the spectral and theoretical evidence suggests the feasibility of energy transfer between adjacent domains.
In order to obtain a visual confirmation about the spatial energy transfer between domains, the photonic propagation behavior was measured in 4@5@NKU-111. The optical waveguide properties of 4@NKU-111 and 5@NKU-111 were first examined to investigate the photonic propagation characteristics of PAH@NKU-111 (Figure S6). These mono PAH@NKU-111 crystals exhibit remarkable optical waveguide performances, which make it possible to realize the energy transfer between adjacent domains in heterogeneous 4@5@NKU-111. In a typical experiment, excitation at different domains in 4@5@NKU-111 was performed and the emission from the tips was measured. As illustrated in Figure 4c and 4d, when the extended 4@NKU-111 domain was excited with a 405 nm laser, photons (blue-green emission centered at 500 nm) were confined and propagated in the 4@NKU-111 domain. At the heterointerface between 4@NKU-111 domain and 5@NKU-111 domain, they were reabsorbed by the pristine 5@NKU-111 domain effectively, and new photons (red emission centered at 680 nm) were generated and continued to propagate along the crystals (indicated with a and b in Figure 4c). This demonstrates that energy transfer has happened at the interface. In comparison, when the irradiation spot was focused on the pristine 5@NKU-111 domain with a 532 nm laser, photons (red emission) were generated and coupled from 5@NKU-111 domain into 4@NKU-111 domain and propagated in the crystals (indicated with c and d). These results are consistent with our expectation that monodirectional photon energy transformation occurs from 4@NKU-111 domain to 5@NKU-111 domain due to the energy gap gradient, while the inverted transformation is not allowed.
In order to further investigate the energy transfer process from 4@NKU-111 domain to 5@NKU-111 domain, fluorescence lifetime imaging microscopy (FLIM) analysis was conducted. First, it is worth noting that the heterointerface in 4@5@NKU-111 involves slight dissolution of the pristine 5@NKU-111 domain together with the nucleation of the extended 4@NKU-111 domain, thereby forming mixed-component in the interface.[37] However, the thickness of the gradient region is only several micrometers and clear-cut segregation of sub-domains with different emissions are unambiguous. This also suggests the advantage of host-guest D-A heterostructure crystals dominated by coordination interaction in domain control accuracy, since organic
