heterocrystals obtained with epitaxial growth usually suffer from unpredictable gradient interface. The FILM image (Figure S7) shows that the PL decay in the extended layer initially remains almost constant (corresponding to the pure 4@NKU-111 domain) and then gradually becomes faster when getting close to the heterointerface (corresponding to the mixed-component in the interface). The average lifetime of extended 4@NKU-111 in the tips was determined to be 26.8 ns, but decreased to 24.6 ns close to the heterointerface. This can be attributed to the resonance mechanism, which behaves as the energy transfer process from 4@NKU-111 component to 5@NKU-111 component.[38-40] These results demonstrate that the effective energy transfer between adjacent domains in heterostructures may occur based on a combination of resonance energy transfer and radiative energy transfer mechanisms depending on the component. In addition, the feasible construction strategy and diverse heterostructures reveal that the spatial energy transfer between domains can be readily controlled and tuned by simply choosing engineering of energy gap gradient by verifying guest donors.
Optical logic gate device prototype based on the heterocrystal. Benefiting from the spatially segregated multicolor emission performance, efficient waveguide behavior, and effective energy transfer, a prototypical optical logic gate device has been proposed in this study. As illustrated in Figure 5a, the crystal of 4@5@NKU-111 could serve as an optical logic gate with multiple input and output ports, where excitation at different positions affords optically guided output signals with certain wavelength. The device consists of two output terminals (output 1 and output 2, indicated with black dotted line boxes. If the light emission at a specific wavelength was observed, the output value was defined as 1; otherwise it was defined as 0.) and two input positions (In 1 and In 2, indicated with red and green dotted line boxes. If the input position was excited, the input value was defined as 1; otherwise it was defined as 0.). As depicted in Figure 5b, when excited at In 1, due to the effective energy transfer, the out-coupled emitting signals output 1 and output 2 are 500 and \(680\;\mathsf{nm}\), respectively. When the excitation spot was focused on In 2, a red emitting signal was transported into output 1 and output 2 (output 1 and output 2 are both \(680\;\mathsf{nm}\)). When excited at both In 1 and In 2, the output signals were 500 and \(680\;\mathsf{nm}\) for output 1, and \(680\;\mathsf{nm}\) for output 2. This designed structure offers a comprehensive function similar with the device in Figure 5c and the corresponding spectra are shown in Figure 5d. The truth table of this optical logic gate function is summarized in Figure 5e. These facile to prepare and diverse heterostructures suggest that more complicated optical networks with the desired functions can be rationally designed and fabricated, thus showing potential for use in integrated photonic logic circuitry.
